.\"                                      Hey, EMACS: -*- nroff -*-
.\" First parameter, NAME, should be all caps
.\" Second parameter, SECTION, should be 1-8, maybe w/ subsection
.\" other parameters are allowed: see man(7), man(1)
.Dd Oct 17, 2020
.Dt PIMD.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pimd.conf
.Nd pimd configuration file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /etc/pimd.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
In many cases you do not need to configure
.Nm pimd .
It configures itself automatically to forward multicast on all
multicast-capable interfaces, i.e., interfaces that have the
.Cm IFF_MULTICAST
flag set.  It locates other PIM-SM/SSM capable routers directly
reachable via those interfaces.
.Pp
.Nm pimd
will not start with less than two enabled virtual interfaces (VIFs).  A
VIF is either a physical multicast-capable interface or a tunnel.  To
override the default settings, for example to disable some interfaces
from being used, configuration commands may be placed in
.Pa /etc/pimd.conf .
.Pp
The file format is relatively free-form: whitespace (including newlines)
is not significant.  However, the order of some statements are
important, more on this below.
.Pp
All
.Ar <len>
arguments to an IPv4 address, group or network can also be given in the
alternative /CIDR format.  E.g.,
.Ar <group>/<len> .
.Pp
By default,
.Nm pimd
runs on all multicast capable interfaces.  The optional
.Cm no phyint
setting and the
.Cm phyint
setting can be used to control this behavior.  More on the
.Cm phyint
interface configuration setting below.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -offset indent -width 1n
.It Cm default-route-distance Ar <1-255>
This option has nothing to do with the system default route.  It is the
default value for the unicast routing protocol's administrative
distance.  It is used in PIM Assert elections to determine upstream
routers.
.Nm pimd
does not take the admin distance from the routing table unless
.Cm assert-preference Cm rib
says to, and cannot take it there at all on a system whose routing
socket does not say which protocol installed a route, so it is
configured here; the metric beside it is read from the kernel routing
table, see
.Cm default-route-metric .
The RFC confusingly refers to this as
.Em metric prefererence .
In a PIM Assert election, the router advertising the lowest assert
preference will be selected as the forwarder and upstream router for the
LAN.  Setting 101 should be sufficiently high so that asserts from Cisco
or GateD routers are preferred over poor-little pimd.
.Pp
It is recommended that distances be set such that metrics are never
consulted.
.It Cm default-route-metric Ar <1-1024>
This setting control the default routing metrics.  Again, this has
nothing to do with the system default route.  This item sets the default
cost for sending data through this router.  You want only PIM-SM data to
go to this daemon; so once again, a high value is recommended to prevent
accidental usage.  The preferred default value is 1024.  Both defaults
can be overridden per
.Cm phyint ,
so learned routes, or PIM Asserts use the
.Cm phyint 's
values.
.Pp
This is a fallback.  A PIM Assert carries the metric of the route to the
source, or to the RP on the shared tree, and
.Nm pimd
takes that from the kernel routing table where the system reports one:
the route priority on Linux, the per route metric on FreeBSD.  This
setting is what is used for a system that reports neither, and it is
then the same number on every router in the domain, leaving the Assert
election to be decided by the address.
.Pp
Please note that PIM Assert elections are not the same as the DR
election.  The PIM Assert election determines the active multicast
forwarder, whereas the DR election determines the active PIM router.
.It Cm autorp Cm discovery Oo Cm enable | Cm disable Oc
Auto-RP, the RP discovery mechanism Cisco published in 1998 and which IOS,
NX-OS and FRR speak.  A domain that runs it has one or more
.Em mapping agents
resolving what the candidate RPs announce and sending the result to
224.0.1.40, where every router listens; the messages are UDP datagrams to
port 496.
.Nm pimd
listens for them by default and puts what it learns in the same RP set the
PIM bootstrap mechanism fills, so
.Ic pimctl show rp
lists both with a
.Cm Type
column saying which is which, and
.Ic pimctl show autorp
shows what was heard and from which agent.
.Pp
Default:
.Cm enable .
Disabling it costs nothing in a domain that runs the bootstrap mechanism and
nothing else, and is the honest setting where both are run and this router
should believe only the BSR.
.Pp
An RP configured here with
.Cm rp-address
always wins over an Auto-RP mapping for the same groups, which is also what
the specification asks for: the two well-known groups need an RP that no
dynamic mapping can take away.
.Pp
.Sy Note:
a
.Em negative
group prefix, which the specification calls a deny, means "these groups are
dense mode" there.
.Nm pimd
has no dense mode, so it reads one as
.Em no RP for these groups ,
and a negative prefix that is the longest match settles the question even
where a shorter positive prefix covers the group.
.Pp
This is the listening half only:
.Nm pimd
does not announce itself as a candidate RP over Auto-RP and is not a
mapping agent, and it does not forward the two well-known groups, so the
messages have to reach it the way the specification assumes -- flooded by
the domain, or carried as ordinary sparse-mode groups with an RP
configured for them.
.It Cm autorp-limit Ar <0-1000000>
How many group-to-RP mappings Auto-RP may make this router hold.  Nothing
authenticates a mapping message and one datagram can name 255 RPs with 255
group prefixes each, so this is a cap on state somebody else creates, like
.Cm rpt-prune-limit
and its neighbours.  Mappings beyond it are refused and the fact is logged
once;
.Ic pimctl show status
has the count.  Default: 1024.
.It Cm assert-preference Oo Cm configured | Cm rib Oc
Where the metric preference of a PIM Assert comes from.  RFC 7761
sec. 4.6.3 says it is the administrative distance of the routing
protocol that provided the route, and
.Cm rib
is that: the kernel is asked which protocol installed the route, and the
distance that protocol is conventionally given is what
.Nm pimd
advertises \(em 0 for a connected route, 1 for a static one, 20 for BGP,
90 for EIGRP, 110 for OSPF, 115 for IS-IS and 120 for RIP, which are the
numbers a Cisco or an Arista on the same LAN advertises for the same
route.  A route whose protocol the kernel does not name, a protocol
with no conventional distance, and every route at all on a system whose
RPF lookups go over a routing socket, keep the
.Cm distance
of the interface.  Default:
.Cm configured ,
which is that number for every route.
.Pp
Only a
.Nm pimd
built
.Fl -enable-netlink ,
which is every Linux build and a FreeBSD one that asked for it, can read
the protocol;
.Ic pimctl show status
prints both the RPF backend and this setting.
.Pp
It is off by default because sec. 4.6.3 compares the preference before
it ever looks at the metric, so a router that derives one cannot be
answered by a router that cannot: two routers on a LAN with the same
routing table, one of them built for netlink and one not, would decide
the election on how they were built rather than on where the traffic
should go.  Turn it on in a domain where every PIM router derives it, or
in none.
.It Cm igmp-query-interval Ar <1-65535>
This setting controls the interval between IGMP querys (QI).  It is used
only when acting as the elected IGMP querier on a LAN.  In IGMP the
lowest numerical address on the LAN becomes the elected querier.  This
value must be same across all IGMP capable devices on the same LAN!
Including any IGMP snooping switches (bridges) that can act as IGMP
querier.
.Pp
The query response interval (QRI) is hard-coded to 10 seconds, so in
practice, the query interval should not be less than that.  Also, at
startup
.Nm
sends the first queries at a quicker interval (QI / 4) to speed up
inital convergence.
.Pp
Default value, per RFC2236: 125
.It Cm igmp-querier-timeout Ar <8-65535>
This setting controls the amount of time that
.Nm pimd
must wait to determine any previous IGMP querier (multicast router) has
been lost.  RFC2236 calls this the "Other Querier Present Interval" and
recommends it be set to a robustness value times the query interval,
plus half the query response time.  The
.Nm pimd
robustness value for IGMP is 3 and the default query response time is 10
sec.  This value must be same across all IGMP capable devices on the
same LAN!  Including any IGMP snooping switches (bridges) that can act
as IGMP querier.
.Pp
When left out, the timeout is calculated from
.Cm igmp-query-interval ,
in which order the two settings appear in the file does not matter.  A
timeout that is not larger than the query interval is refused and the
calculated one used instead, a timeout below the recommended value is
warned about but honored.
.Pp
Default value, per RFC2236: 380
.It Cm hello-interval Ar <30-18724>
The PIM Hello message interval can be tuned by changing this setting.
It also affects the hold-time value included in Hello messages.  The
hold-time value is 3.5 times hello-interval.  Anything less than 30 sec
is considered an "aggressive" setting and is unsupported: a value outside
the range above is refused, with a warning, and the default used instead.
.Pp
Default value: 30 sec.
.It Cm rpt-prune-limit Ar <0-1000000>
The most (S,G) routing entries that PIM neighbors' Prune(S,G,rpt)
messages may make
.Nm pimd
hold, counting both those addressed to it and those it overhears on its
upstream interface.  Every such Prune names a source of the neighbor's
choosing and keeps an entry for as long as the neighbor's HoldTime asks,
so without a limit one neighbor decides how much state the router
holds, and how long the lists every Join/Prune is looked up in grow.
.Pp
Once the limit is reached a Prune(S,G,rpt) addressed to
.Nm pimd
for a source it holds no entry for is not applied, so that source keeps
being forwarded on the interface, and one overheard upstream is
overridden with an early Join(*,G) rather than a Join(S,G,rpt).  A
warning is logged the first time, and
.Cm pimctl show status
reports the entries held.  0 makes no such entries at all.
.Pp
Default value: 1024
.It Cm local-sg-limit Ar <0-1000000>
The most (S,G) routing entries that data from directly connected sources
may make
.Nm pimd
hold on the interfaces it is the designated router for.  Every packet to
a group with no entry yet makes one, and a kernel cache entry beside it,
and a host on the subnet chooses both the group and, within the subnet,
the source address, so without a limit one host decides how much state
the router holds.
.Pp
Once the limit is reached a source and group
.Nm pimd
holds no entry for is not registered with the RP, so its traffic is not
forwarded beyond its own LAN, and each of its packets stays a kernel
cache miss.  Entries already held are refreshed as before.  A warning is
logged the first time, and
.Cm pimctl show status
reports the entries held.  Raise it on a designated router for more
sources and groups than the default.  0 makes no such entries at all.
.Pp
Default value: 4096
.It Cm register-sg-limit Ar <0-1000000>
The most (S,G) routing entries that PIM Register messages may make
.Nm pimd
hold as a rendezvous point.  Each Register names a source and a group of
its sender's choosing, is unicast from anywhere that can reach the RP,
and can claim any source address, including that of another member of an
.Cm anycast-rp
set, whose copies make state and join the source tree as well.  Without a
limit, whoever can reach the RP decides how much state it holds.
.Pp
Once the limit is reached a Register for a source and group
.Nm pimd
holds no entry for makes none: the Register-Stop it is owed is still sent,
but no keepalive is kept and no copy leads to a join.  Entries already held
are refreshed as before.  A warning is logged the first time, and
.Cm pimctl show status
reports the entries held.  Raise it on an RP for more active sources than
the default.  0 makes no such entries at all.
.Pp
Default value: 4096
.It Cm no phyint
This setting controls if
.Nm pimd
should start up with all multicast-capable interfaces as
.Em enabled
or
.Em disabled .
For more information, see the description of
.Cm phyint ,
below.
.It Cm phyint Ar <address | ifname> Oo Cm disable | enable Oc Oo Cm igmpv2 | igmpv3 Oc Oo Cm dr-priority Ar <1-4294967294> Oc Oo Cm ttl-threshold Ar <1-255> Oc Oo Cm distance Ar <1-255> Oc Oo Cm metric Ar <1-1024> Oc Oo Cm altnet Ar network Ns / Ns Ar len | Ar network Cm masklen Ar len Oc Oo Cm accept-nbr-from Ar address Ns / Ns Ar len | Ar address Cm masklen Ar len Oc Oo Cm scoped Ar network Ns / Ns Ar len | Ar network Cm masklen Ar len Oc
.Pp
This setting selects and alters properties of the phyiscal interfaces
.Nm pimd
operates on.  Interfaces can be identified using their local IP address
or their name.
.Pp
An interface that does not exist yet may be named here.
.Nm pimd
rescans the kernel interface list while it runs, and applies the
.Cm phyint
line naming an interface when that interface appears, so a link that is
negotiated rather than configured, such as a PPP or L2TP link or a tunnel
that comes up, is configured from this file like any other.  It is
reported at start-up as an unknown phyint name, once.
.Pp
.Sy NOTE: All
.Cm phyint
commands must come after the
.Cm default-route-metric
and
.Cm default-route-distance
settings in the configuration file.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -offset indent -width 1n
.It Cm disable | Cm enable
Selectively disable or enable this interface.  Only enabled interfaces
get a VIF in the kernel.
.It Cm igmpv2 | Cm igmpv3
Force interface to use IGMPv2 or IGMPv3.  Default:
.Cm igmpv3
since v2.3.0.
.It Cm dr-priority Ar <1-4294967294>
When there are multiple PIM routers on the same LAN the DR is usually
elected based on the highest numerical IP address.  This setting can be
used to control the DR Priority option in PIM Hellow messages, which by
default otherwise is 1.  When the DR Priority option is advertised by
.Em all
PIM routers on the same LAN the highest priority router wins the DR
election, regardless of its IP.  If any router does
.Em not
advertise the DR Priority option, or the same priority is advertised by
more than one router, the protocol falls back to using the IP address.
.It Cm ttl-threshold Ar <1-255>
The TTL threshold for multicast frames to be forwarded from this
interface.  Useful for defining boundaries for local, site, and global
multicast.  This is often referred to as TTL scoping.  Default: 1
.It Cm distance Ar <1-255>
Use this to override the
.Cm default-route-distance
(101) on this
.Cm phyint
in PIM Assert elections.
.It Cm metric Ar <1-1024>
The cost of sending data through this interface, for sources reached
over it.  Like
.Cm default-route-metric ,
which it defaults to (1024), this is only consulted where the kernel
reports no metric for the route itself.
.It Cm altnet Ar <network/len>
Alternative host(s)/network(s) to accept as locally attached multicast
sources on a given interface. If a phyint is attached to multiple IP
subnets, describe each additional subnet with the altnet keyword.
.Pp
Every address the interface already carries is added this way on its own:
.Nm pimd
can only create one VIF per interface, so when it finds a second address
it keeps that subnet as an altnet of the VIF the first one made.  This
setting is for subnets reached over the interface without an address on
it, and for overriding the prefix length of one that has.
.It Cm accept-nbr-from Ar <address[/len]>
The routers this interface accepts PIM messages from.  RFC 7761 sec. 6.2
asks for such an option, and without it every router that sends a
syntactically valid Hello on a subnet
.Nm pimd
has an interface on becomes a neighbor of it, and from there can take the
DR role, take part in the assert election, and have its Joins believed.
.Pp
Repeatable on one
.Cm phyint
line, and with no length given the address is one router rather than a
prefix.  An interface with no
.Cm accept-nbr-from
accepts every router, which the same section requires of every option of
this kind, and which is what keeps a half-written filter from black-holing
a domain.  Refusing a router's Hello is what does the work: a Join/Prune,
an Assert and a unicast Bootstrap are all refused from a router no Hello
has been accepted from.
.Pp
This is address-based, so it is worth only as much as the addresses on the
link are: it keeps an unconfigured or misconfigured router out, and keeps
an attacker to the addresses it can source from.  RFC 7761 sec. 6.3's
IPsec is the other half, and
.Nm pimd
neither installs nor requires a security association.
.It Cm scoped Ar <network/len>
Optional scoping of multicast groups.  This allows interfaces to be
configured as an administrative boundary for the specified group(s).
Multicast streams belonging to the scoped groups will not be forwarded.
.El
.It Cm bsr-candidate Oo Ar address | Ar ifname Oc Oo Cm priority Ar <0-255> Oc
The
.Cm bsr-candidate
(CBSR) setting is enabled in the default configuration file.  It can be
disabled by commenting it out, but make sure at least one bootstrap
router (BSR) is available in the network.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -offset indent -width 1n
.It Cm address | ifname
Optional local IPv4 address, or interface name to acquire address from.
If both address and ifname is left out,
.Nm pimd
will default to the highest active IP address.
.It Cm priority Ar <0-255>
How important this router is compared to others.  A larger numeric value
denotes higher priority, c.f.
.Cm rp-candidate
where it's the reverese.
.El
.It Cm rp-candidate Oo Ar address | Ar ifname Oc Oo Cm priority Ar <0-255> Oc Oo Cm interval Ar <10-16384> Oc Oo Cm group-prefix Ar group Ns / Ns Ar len | Cm masklen Ar len Oc
The
.Cm rp-candidate
(CRP) setting is enabled in the default configuration file.  It can be
disabled by commenting out the line, but make sure there is at least one
Rendez-vous Point (RP) in the network for PIM-SM.  See
.Cm rp-address
(below) for how to configure a static RP.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -offset indent -width 1n
.It Cm address | ifname
Optional local IPv4 address, or interface name to acquire address from.
If both address and ifname is left out,
.Nm pimd
will default to the highest active IP address.
.It Cm priority Ar <0-255>
A smaller numeric value denotes higher priorty, c.f.
.Cm bsr-candidate
where it's the reverse.  If the priority is omitted
.Nm pimd
and Cisco IOS default to 0, the standard says 192 for RP.
.It Cm interval Ar <10-16383>
The advertisement interval in seconds for this CRP.  The default value
is 30 seconds.  Use a lower value for faster convergence.
.It Cm group-prefix Oo Oo Ar group Ns / Ns Ar len Oc | Oo Ar group Cm masklen Ar len Oc Oc
The
.Cm group-prefix
option is the set of multicast groups that the CRP will advertise to
other routers, if it wins an election:
.Bl -tag -offset indent -width 1n
.It Ar group Ns / Ns Ar len
A specific multicast group, or network range in CIDR syntax this router
will handle.
.It Ar group Cm masklen Ar len
Optional number of groups, in prefix length format. Remember that a
multicast address is a Class D and has a netmask of 240.0.0.0, which
means its length is 4.
.El
.Pp
Multiple lines of
.Cm group-prefix
may be given, but the maximum number of records supported in
.Nm pimd
is 255.
.El
.It Cm rp-address Ar address Oo Ar group-addr Ns / Ns Ar len | Ar group-addr Cm masklen Ar len Oc
This setting is for static rendezvous point (RP) configurations.  It
defines the RP for a given group, or range of groups.  The argument can
be either a unicast address or a multicast group, with an optional group
address and netmask.  Default group and netmask is 224.0.0.0/16.
.Sy Note:
all static RP's are announced with priority 1 (second highest, see above).
.It Cm register-accept-from Ar address Ns / Ns Ar len | Ar address Cm masklen Ar len
The routers this rendezvous point (RP) accepts PIM Register messages
from.  A Register is unicast, so without this any host that can reach the
RP can have traffic of its choosing decapsulated onto a shared tree the
RP is the root of, which is the attack RFC 7761 sec. 6.1.2 describes.
The same section's sec. 6.2 asks for this option and requires it to
default to accepting every sender, so a configuration without this
setting accepts Registers from anywhere, as earlier versions always did.
.Pp
Multiple lines may be given; a sender matching any of them is accepted
and the rest are ignored without an answer, since a Register-Stop would
tell a forged sender that it reached the RP.  A prefix length may be left
out, in which case it is 32, a single router.
.Pp
.Sy Note:
this matches the address the Register was sent from, i.e. the designated
router that encapsulated the packet, and not the source address of the
packet inside it.  Cisco's
.Cm ip pim accept-register
is the latter and is a different control.  Note also that an attacker
able to spoof the source address of a unicast packet is not stopped by
this, only one that cannot.
.Pp
.Sy Important:
this setting reaches the control plane only.  Decapsulating a Register is
the kernel's work, not
.Nm pimd Ns 's,
and the kernel loops the inner packet back on the register interface
before the daemon is handed the header to act on.  What this setting
refuses is the multicast routing state, the keepalive it would refresh
and the Register-Stop that would be sent back \(em not the encapsulated
packet, which has already been forwarded if the group has a shared tree
to forward it on.  An RP that must keep forged traffic off that tree
needs a packet filter for IP protocol 103 in front of it; this setting
does not replace one.
.It Cm anycast-rp Ar anycast-address Ar member-address
One member of an Anycast-RP set, RFC 4610: several rendezvous points
sharing one RP address, the
.Ar anycast-address ,
each with a unique address of its own.  Every member holds the anycast
address, usually as a /32 on a loopback interface, and unicast routing
takes each designated router's Registers to the nearest member.  That
member copies every Register it receives from outside the set to all the
other members, so that a receiver joined at any of them is reached by
every source, with no MSDP between the members.  A member that is itself
the designated router of a source is sent no Register to copy, and
registers the source to the other members instead, from its unique
address, honouring their Register-Stops as a designated router does.
.Pp
A member copies at most 256 Registers a second, over all its sets, to the
other members, and at most 64 of those whole with their data; past 64 a
copy is a Null-Register, which carries the source to the other members
without the packet, and past 256 there is no copy and a warning is logged.
RFC 4610 leaves rate limiting to the designated router, which a sender that
is not one does not do, so every accepted Register would otherwise be sent
on once per member.  The state Registers make on a member is capped by
.Cm register-sg-limit .
.Pp
Give one line per member, this router's own unique address included, so
that the same lines can be used on every member.  At most 8 members per
anycast address.  This router is the member whose
.Ar member-address
is the primary address of one of its PIM interfaces; a secondary address
does not count.
.Pp
For the set to work, on every member: the anycast address must be the RP
for the groups, from
.Cm rp-address
or
.Cm rp-candidate ,
and an address of the router; a
.Cm register-accept-from
list, if any, must cover the other members' unique addresses as well as
the designated routers; and
.Cm bsr-candidate
must not use the anycast address, which several routers hold.
.Nm pimd
warns at startup and on reload when the anycast address is not local, when
no member or more than one is, when
.Cm bsr-candidate
uses the anycast address, and when a member is outside
.Cm register-accept-from .
.Pp
.Sy Note:
where this differs from RFC 4610.  On FreeBSD the kernel hands
.Nm pimd
only the headers of a Register carrying data, so the copy sent to the
other members is a Null-Register, and receivers behind them miss the
packets sent before their RP joins the source tree.  A copy's TTL is the
received TTL less one, rather than the same TTL, so that a copy cannot
circulate between members with inconsistent sets.  A member that receives
a copy and has receivers for the group joins the source tree whatever
.Cm spt-threshold
says: the member that sent the copy answers the designated router by its
own state, and stops it with a Register-Stop as soon as it has no receivers
of its own, after which no data would reach the others.
.Pp
Members running Arista EOS interoperate, tested with 4.36.1F under vEOS in
both directions: copies, Null-Register copies, and the Registers of a
member that is the designated router of a source.  EOS requires its own
member address to be a /32 on a loopback interface; configured with any
other address it accepts the set and silently never acts on it.
.It Cm spt-threshold Oo Cm rate Ar KBPS | Cm packets Ar NUM | Cm infinity Oc Oo Cm interval Ar SEC Oc
This replaces two previous configuration settings:
.Cm switch_data_threshold
and
.Cm switch_register_threshold .
It controls the switch-over from the shared tree to the shortest-path
source tree.  The default is to do the switch-over after the first
packet, but only after 100 seconds.  If
.Ar infinity
is specified the shortest path switch-over is disabled.
.It Cm ssm-range Oo Cm default | Oo Ar group Ns / Ns Ar len Oc | Oo Ar group Cm masklen Ar len Oc Oc
The set of multicast groups
.Nm pimd
handles as Source Specific Multicast, i.e. groups for which it builds a
source tree per (S,G) reported by a receiver and never a shared tree.
The default, when no
.Cm ssm-range
is given, is 232.0.0.0/8 from RFC 4607.
.Bl -tag -offset indent -width 1n
.It Cm default
The default range, 232.0.0.0/8.
.It Ar group Ns / Ns Ar len
A multicast group, or a range of them in CIDR syntax.
.It Ar group Cm masklen Ar len
The same, with the prefix length given separately.
.El
.Pp
Multiple lines of
.Cm ssm-range
may be given.  The configured ranges
.Sy replace
the default rather than adding to it, like Cisco's
.Cm ip pim ssm range ,
so keeping 232.0.0.0/8 alongside a range of your own takes an explicit
.Cm ssm-range default
line.  A range covering the link-local groups, 224.0.0.0/24, is rejected.
.Pp
.Sy Note:
this changes how a group behaves, not just how it is named.  Receivers on
a group that becomes source specific have to ask for their sources, with
IGMPv3, because an any-source report for an SSM group is ignored, RFC
4604.  Conversely, groups of a default range that has been replaced go
back to needing an RP.  All routers in the domain should agree on the
ranges: nothing in PIM advertises them.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/lib/pimd.genid -compact
.It Pa /etc/pimd.conf
Main configuration file.
